concern:
  heading: "Patient information: where this stops"
  body: >
    <p>Health information is sensitive information under the Privacy Act and carries
    obligations most other sectors don't have. So we'll be blunt about it: identifiable
    patient information does not belong in a consumer AI tool, and any provider telling
    you otherwise is not worth listening to.</p>
    <p>What the program covers is the practical version of that line — which tools carry
    the contractual terms that make business use defensible, what has to be de-identified
    and how to do it reliably, which tasks should stay entirely inside your practice
    software, and how to write a policy your team follows. We also cover the failure mode
    that matters clinically: output that reads authoritatively and is wrong. Every
    workflow we teach has a named human who verifies before anything reaches a patient
    or a file.</p>
    <p>This is training in how to use the tools safely. It is not legal or clinical
    advice, and your practice remains responsible for its own privacy and professional
    obligations — we'd expect your practice manager to sign off the workflow before it
    goes live.</p>
faq:
  - q: "Can AI write clinical notes?"
    a: "Dedicated clinical scribe products exist and some practices use them, but that is a different purchase with its own compliance questions. Our program is about the practice's own capability across administration, correspondence and patient communication — which is the larger, less served part of the workload."
  - q: "Is this safe with patient data?"
    a: "Only with the right tool tier and the right workflow, which is precisely what the training establishes. The honest answer is that safety here is a function of setup and habit, not of the tool alone — and most practices currently have neither, while staff use these tools anyway."
  - q: "Who in the practice should do this?"
    a: "Practice managers and reception get the most immediate return, because their work is highest in volume and lowest in clinical risk. Clinicians usually join for the correspondence and patient-explanation modules. You don't need to send everyone."
  - q: "We're a dental practice, not a GP clinic. Does it still apply?"
    a: "Yes. Dental, allied health, specialist and veterinary practices all have the same administrative shape — referrals, recalls, patient explanation and policy upkeep. The examples in the program are adjusted to the practices in the cohort."
  - q: "How long before we see anything?"
    a: "Correspondence drafting and patient explanations usually land in the first fortnight, because they are frequent and immediately noticeable. The bigger workflow changes take the full three months to bed in."
